{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310668,"nonce":"mtlIg9pHdNVCeX7J0pcRkUYvnT4=","title":"OSR transition is NOT the cause of test_stringbuf_stringtools SIGILL under multi-var. Interpreter→JIT promotion uses an interpreted ...","message":"When test_stringbuf_stringtools SIGILLs pre-main under multi-var RD support and cumulative suite cache, is the interpreter→JIT transition (OSR-style promotion) responsible for stranding NaN-boxed function ids in vtables that JIT code then dispatches through?\n\n\nTwo independent guards close the window:\n\n1. **The interpreter's CallIndirect handler decodes its own NaN-boxed values.** [mir_interpreter.rs:1594-1604](compiler/src/codegen/mir_interpreter.rs#L1594-L1604) checks `(ptr_val.0 \u0026 TAG_MASK) == TAG_FUNC` and dispatches by `IrFunctionId` *without dereferencing the value as a native function pointer*. So interpreter execution of virtual calls is safe even though vtable slots written via `haxe_vtable_set_slot` contain `NanBoxedValue::from_func_id(id).0` from `interp_to_native` at [mir_interpreter.rs:3151](compiler/src/codegen/mir_interpreter.rs#L3151) (the `to_function_bits()` fallback in the `IrType::I64` arm).\n\n2. **JIT compilation re-runs `__vtable_init__` natively before any JIT code executes.** [tiered_backend.rs:1501-1504](compiler/src/codegen/tiered_backend.rs#L1501-L1504) (`rerun_loaded_modules_startup_jit`) is called at the *end* of `compile_all_modules_jit` ([tiered_backend.rs:2008](compiler/src/codegen/tiered_backend.rs#L2008)), which calls the native `__vtable_init__` for every loaded module via `run_startup_hooks_native` at [tiered_backend.rs:1445-1469](compiler/src/codegen/tiered_backend.rs#L1445-L1469). The native version overwrites every slot the interpreter touched with the real `cranelift backend.get_function_ptr()`.\n\nThe window between `ensure_loaded_modules_initialized` (interpreter pollution) and `compile_all_modules_jit` end (native cleanup) is purely compile-time work — no Haxe code runs in it, so no virtual dispatch can observe the polluted slots.\n\n\n`compile_file_with_shared_state_ex` at [compilation.rs:4870-4905](compiler/src/compilation.rs#L4870-L4905) (renumber pass) and [compilation.rs:4972-5013](compiler/src/compilation.rs#L4972-L5013) (stub→real replacement) only rewrite three instruction kinds: `CallDirect`, `FunctionRef`, `MakeClosure`. Same coverage in `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` at [compilation.rs:3294-3361](compiler/src/compilation.rs#L3294-L3361) and `renumber_and_push_import_mir`.\n\nAudit confirmed nothing else carries `IrFunctionId`:\n- `IrInstruction::Switch.cases: Vec\u003c(IrValue, IrId)\u003e` — `IrValue` is the match value (Int/String/etc), never a function id.\n- `IrValue::Function(IrFunctionId)` exists ([ir/types.rs:366](compiler/src/ir/types.rs#L366)) but the production code path uses `FunctionRef` instructions exclusively (explicit comment at [hir_to_mir.rs:8140-8150](compiler/src/ir/hir_to_mir.rs#L8140-L8150)) because indirect calls expect `{fn_ptr,env_ptr}` closure layout — `IrValue::Function` as a `Const` would produce a bare fn_addr that SIGSEGVs when CallIndirect reads it as a closure.\n- `IrTerminator` variants carry block ids, not function ids.\n\nSo the three-kind coverage is complete for the current MIR shape.\n\n\n`parse_block_expr` in [parser/src/rd/expr.rs:1486-1499](parser/src/rd/expr.rs#L1486-L1499) is reverted to single-expression-per-element. With multi-var disabled, Int64.hx still falls through to the legacy nom parser via the warning at [haxe_parser.rs:108-111](parser/src/haxe_parser.rs#L108-L111). 128/128 haxe regression PASS at HEAD (verified 2026-05-26).\n\n\nDon't waste cycles on OSR / tier-promotion races as the next investigation step for the multi-var crash. Look instead at:\n\n1. **BLADE cache content shape.** Specifically why cumulative cache state (100+ prior tests) is necessary for reproduction. Either a cached MIR shape carries a stub id that survives the three-instruction-kind rewriter (unlikely — see audit above), or the build_id check has a hole that lets cross-build cache pollution through.\n\n2. **Diff cached .blade files for StringTools across the multi-var vs no-multi-var runs.** If the only delta is internal MIR register IDs (expected from RD shape changes), the issue is downstream of the cache. If the cached function IDs differ in ways that affect dispatch targets, look at how renumbering interacts with import ordering.\n\n3. **Stub name matching at compilation.rs:4938.** The replacement loop matches `func.name`. If multi-var causes any synthetic helper to be named differently than the stdlib expects (e.g. `string_concat` vs `String_concat` casing), the stub stays unresolved. Worth grepping `[fixup] stub detected` output for any name in `id_replacements` that doesn't have a corresponding stdlib impl with the same casing.\n\nRelated: [[bugs-int64-multivar-stub-replace]] (the umbrella bug doc), [[bugs-forward-ref-stub-unresolved]] (prior cross-file dispatch fix that touched the same name-first resolution path), [[bugs-blade-stale-cross-module]] (build_id gate that closed the cross-build leak).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_osr_analysis_multivar` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}